                    briefing_title_r2:      `Round 2 — Demand the right analysis`,
                    briefing_text_r2:       `Now rebuild the case on defensible assumptions. Replace the 6% growth <strong>anchor</strong> with a scenario range (base 1.5%, upside 3%) and run sensitivity. Strip the €1.4M sunk cost out of the decision entirely — it is gone whether you approve or kill the project. Model a realistic <strong>9–14 month ramp</strong> (comparable installs cost ~€2.8M in transition). And decide what to do about the vendor's €7.6M/yr savings: the vendor earns €24M if you say yes, so is it worth €40K and 3 days of your clock to get an <em>independent</em> estimate?`,
                    briefing_title_r3:      `Round 3 — Confront the sponsor`,
                    briefing_text_r3:       `The COO is across the table — the founder's nephew, a board favourite, and he does not want to hear “wait.” Deliver your findings without either <strong>capitulating</strong> or <strong>detonating</strong> the relationship. Choose your stance, decide whether to lead with evidence or pull rank, and handle the sunk-cost plea (“we've already invested €1.4M”). The relationship matters — but protect it too hard and you have simply folded. Use the corrected numbers, not your authority.`,
                    briefing_title_r4:      `Round 4 — Escalate or endorse`,
                    briefing_text_r4:       `Decide what goes to the supervisory board <strong>under controlling's name</strong>. Sign the original €24M (a write-down would land near a 3.0x net-debt/EBITDA covenant test), sign a corrected/phased €9M pilot with milestone gates, or formally escalate a documented dissent. Weigh a wrong “no” (lost credibility, blocked value) against a wrong “yes” (a €24M write-down at the worst moment). And design the <em>rationality safeguards</em> that should govern every future capex sign-off.`,

                    r1_approach_label:      `Where does controlling's work start on this dossier?`,
                    appr_biasfirst:         `Catalogue the biases first — detect the reasoning failures before recomputing anything`,
                    appr_recompute:         `Rebuild the NPV immediately on your own assumptions`,
                    appr_accept:            `Accept the COO's framing and verify a few figures later`,
                    r1_topbias_label:       `Which single bias does the most damage — the one that flips the case if uncaught?`,
                    bias_optimism:          `Optimism — the 6% volume-growth assumption vs. 1.5% in the order book and 5-year actuals`,
                    bias_anchoring:         `Anchoring — the €7.6M savings taken straight from the vendor's sales deck`,
                    bias_sunk:              `Sunk cost — the €1.4M already spent on the feasibility study and pilot cell`,
                    bias_none:              `No material bias — the model looks internally consistent`,
                    r1_rigor_label:         `How rigorously do you tie each flagged bias to a specific line in the NPV model? (%)`,
                    r1_rigor_readout:       `Evidential rigor of the bias register:`,

                    r2_growth_label:        `What do you do with the 6% volume-growth anchor?`,
                    grow_scenario:          `Replace it with a scenario range — base 1.5%, upside 3% — and run sensitivity`,
                    grow_flat3:             `Use a single 3% figure as a compromise with the COO`,
                    grow_keep6:             `Keep the 6% — the COO knows the order book better than you`,
                    r2_sunk_label:          `How do you treat the €1.4M already spent on the feasibility study and pilot?`,
                    sunk_strip:             `Strip it out entirely — it is gone whether you approve or kill the project`,
                    sunk_partial:           `Count part of it — some of the work carries forward`,
                    sunk_keep:              `Let it count — “we can't waste what we've already invested”`,
                    r2_savings_label:       `What do you do about the vendor-sourced €7.6M/yr savings figure?`,
                    sav_independent:        `Commission an independent estimate (€40K, 3 days) — the vendor earns €24M if you say yes`,
                    sav_discount:           `Discount it judgmentally toward ~€4.9M without an external check`,
                    sav_accept:             `Accept the €7.6M to save three days on the clock`,
                    r2_ramp_label:          `How many months of ramp-up to steady state do you model? (vendor assumes month 1)`,
                    r2_ramp_hint:           `Comparable installations needed 9–14 months and ~€2.8M in transition cost. Modelling month-1 full savings imports the optimism bias.`,

                    r3_stance_label:        `You face the COO. What stance do you take into the conversation?`,
                    stance_conditional:     `Conditional yes — require a phased €9M Plant 2 pilot first, with savings milestones to release the rest`,
                    stance_clearno:         `A clear no, backed by the corrected numbers`,
                    stance_redesign:        `Send it back for redesign on defensible assumptions`,
                    stance_capitulate:      `Endorse the original — he is the founder's nephew and the board likes him`,
                    r3_evidence_label:      `When the COO pushes back, what do you lead with?`,
                    evi_evidence:           `Evidence — the corrected NPV, the sensitivity table and the independence problem`,
                    evi_authority:          `Authority — the CFO mandated this sign-off and you have the final word`,
                    evi_defer:              `Defer — he understands operations better, so you soften your findings`,
                    r3_plea_label:          `The COO plays the sunk-cost card: “we've already invested €1.4M.” How do you handle it?`,
                    plea_reject:            `Reject it cleanly — the €1.4M is irrelevant to today's go/no-go decision`,
                    plea_accommodate:       `Acknowledge it as a factor without letting it decide`,
                    plea_concede:           `Concede — finishing what was started does carry weight`,
                    r3_tone_label:          `Relationship management: how hard do you protect the working relationship with the sponsor? (%)`,
                    r3_tone_readout:        `Care for the relationship (too low detonates it, too high becomes capitulation):`,

                    r4_signoff_label:       `What goes to the supervisory board under controlling's name?`,
                    sign_corrected:         `Sign a corrected, phased Wirtschaftlichkeitsnachweis — €9M pilot with milestone gates`,
                    sign_escalate:          `Formally escalate a documented dissent to the CFO and board`,
                    sign_original:          `Sign the original €24M proposal as submitted`,
                    r4_phased_label:        `How do you structure the commitment?`,
                    phase_milestones:       `Stage it — milestone gates that release capital only as savings are proven`,
                    phase_allornothing:     `All-or-nothing — €24M now or kill it outright`,
                    r4_governance_label:    `What rationality safeguard do you propose for ALL future capex sign-offs?`,
                    gov_template:           `A sign-off template that strips sunk costs and forces independent verification + sensitivity`,
                    gov_review:             `An annual post-mortem review of past approvals`,
                    gov_none:               `No new governance — this was a one-off test`,
                    r4_conviction_label:    `Conviction: how firmly do you stand behind your recommendation to the board? (%)`,

                    warn_rigor_range:       `Evidential rigor must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_ramp_range:        `Ramp-up must be between 0 and 14 months.`,
                    warn_tone_range:        `Relationship care must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,
                    warn_conviction_range:  `Conviction must be a percentage between 0 and 100.`,

                    coach_default:          `Decision logged.`,
                    coach_r1_biasfirst:     `<strong>Right sequence.</strong> Controlling's first job is to expose the reasoning failures, not to produce a rival forecast. Detect the biases, then compute — that is rationality assurance, not bookkeeping.`,
                    coach_r1_recompute:     `<strong>You've jumped to a new number.</strong> Good — but the COO will ask “whose assumptions, yours or mine?” If you can't first NAME why his 6% growth and €7.6M savings are wrong, you're just two analysts disagreeing. Expose the bias before you compute.`,
                    coach_r1_accept:        `<strong>You accepted his framing.</strong> That is exactly how a sign-off becomes a rubber stamp. Controlling exists to challenge the case, not to ratify it and check a few cells.`,
                    coach_r1_diag_sharp:    `<strong>Sharp diagnosis.</strong> You detected before computing AND named optimism as the case-flipping bias — the 6% growth against a 1.5% order book is what turns +€5.2M into −€3.8M. Tie it to the volume line and you have a defensible critique, not a hunch.`,
                    coach_r1_anchoring:     `<strong>Anchoring is real</strong> — €7.6M lifted from the vendor's deck is a serious flaw, a 36% haircut. But it isn't the bias that single-handedly flips the case: the 6% growth optimism does. Rank by which line moves the NPV the most.`,
                    coach_r1_sunk:          `<strong>The €1.4M sunk cost is a trap</strong> — but it is logically irrelevant to today's decision, so it can't be the bias that flips the NPV. The case-flipping error is the optimistic 6% growth. Don't mistake the most emotive bias for the most material one.`,
                    coach_r1_none:          `<strong>“Internally consistent” is how you get the rubber stamp.</strong> A model built on a vendor's savings deck and 6% growth against a 1.5% order book is not consistent — it is optimistic and anchored. There absolutely is a material bias.`,
                    coach_r1_lowrigor:      `<strong>A bias register with no evidence is just a feeling.</strong> If you can't tie “optimism” to the volume line and “anchoring” to the savings line, the COO will dismiss it. Rigor is what turns “this feels off” into a defensible challenge.`,
                    coach_r2_scenario:      `<strong>That's reference-class forecasting.</strong> A scenario range anchored on the 1.5% order book rather than the champion's 6% is the correction that matters most — it is the assumption that flips +€5.2M to −€3.8M. Now run sensitivity on it.`,
                    coach_r2_flat3:         `<strong>A single 3% is a negotiated number, not an analysed one.</strong> Splitting the difference with the COO feels collaborative but it still embeds optimism. The discipline is a base-1.5% / upside-3% range with sensitivity, not a compromise point estimate.`,
                    coach_r2_keep6:         `<strong>You kept the 6%.</strong> “The COO knows the order book” is precisely the deference rationality assurance exists to counteract — the order book itself shows 1.5%. Keep the anchor and your corrected NPV is still his.`,
                    coach_r2_strip:         `<strong>Sunk-cost discipline.</strong> The €1.4M is gone whether you approve or kill the project, so it is correctly irrelevant to the €24M go/no-go. Stripping it out keeps his bias from contaminating controlling's own analysis.`,
                    coach_r2_partial:       `<strong>“Some of it carries forward” is the sunk-cost fallacy in disguise.</strong> Either a cost is recoverable from a future decision or it is gone; the €1.4M feasibility-and-pilot spend is gone. Counting part of it still tilts the decision toward yes.`,
                    coach_r2_keepsunk:      `<strong>You imported his sunk-cost bias.</strong> “We can't waste what we've invested” is the textbook trap — the €1.4M is irrelevant to today's decision. By letting it count you've made controlling's analysis as biased as the proposal.`,
                    coach_r2_independent:   `<strong>Independence over speed.</strong> €40K and 3 days to replace a number sold to you by the party that earns €24M is the cheapest insurance in the dossier — the independent estimate lands at €4.9M, a 36% gap that flips the case. That is what your name is worth.`,
                    coach_r2_discount:      `<strong>A judgmental haircut is better than nothing</strong> — but you're still guessing. ~€4.9M happens to be close to the independent figure, yet without the external check the COO can argue your number is as arbitrary as his. The €40K buys you defensibility.`,
                    coach_r2_acceptsav:     `<strong>You signed your name to the vendor's number.</strong> €7.6M came straight from the deck of the party that earns €24M if you say yes; the independent estimate is €4.9M. Saving three days here is the most expensive shortcut in the case.`,
                    coach_r2_shortramp:     `<strong>A short ramp imports the optimism bias.</strong> The vendor assumes full savings from month 1; comparable installations needed 9–14 months and ~€2.8M in transition cost. A 1–4 month ramp still overstates the early cash flows.`,
                    coach_r2_noramp:        `<strong>Month-1 full savings is the vendor's fantasy.</strong> No automation line hits steady state instantly — comparable installs took 9–14 months and €2.8M in transition. Model the ramp or your NPV is as optimistic as his.`,
                    coach_r3_conditional:   `<strong>Yes, but prove it at €9M first.</strong> A phased Plant 2 pilot with savings milestones converts an irreversible €24M bet into a real option — it lets you say “prove it” instead of forcing a flat no. That is the bravest and smartest call here.`,
                    coach_r3_clearno:       `<strong>A clear no on corrected numbers is defensible</strong> — but it is binary. With a covenant test looming and the COO possibly partly right, a phased €9M pilot would let you de-risk without blocking all the value. Was a flat no the only option?`,
                    coach_r3_redesign:      `<strong>Sending it back buys time but dodges the call.</strong> The board votes in days; “redesign it” without your own corrected case risks looking like controlling can critique but not decide. Pair it with a phased recommendation.`,
                    coach_r3_capitulate:    `<strong>You folded.</strong> You endorsed the original because he's the founder's nephew and the board likes him. Eighteen months on the line is at 60% of promised savings, there's a €24M write-down, and it hits your 3.0x covenant test. What is controlling FOR if it caves to a strong personality?`,
                    coach_r3_authority:     `<strong>Pulling rank loses the room.</strong> “The CFO mandated this” wins the meeting and loses the relationship — and the moment your evidence is thin, authority is all you have left. Lead with the corrected NPV and the independence problem, not the mandate.`,
                    coach_r3_defer:         `<strong>Softening your findings is quiet capitulation.</strong> “He understands operations better” is the deference rationality assurance exists to counteract. You can respect his operating knowledge and still hold the line on the numbers.`,
                    coach_r3_accommodate:   `<strong>“A factor, but not decisive” still lets the sunk cost in the room.</strong> The €1.4M is irrelevant to the go/no-go; acknowledging it as a factor concedes ground you didn't need to. Reject it cleanly and pivot to the forward NPV.`,
                    coach_r3_concede:       `<strong>You conceded the sunk-cost plea.</strong> “Finishing what we started” is the fallacy itself — the €1.4M is gone regardless. Concede it and you've let his bias decide controlling's position.`,
                    coach_r3_detonate:      `<strong>You detonated the relationship.</strong> Being right is not enough if the sponsor will never work with controlling again — the function needs this credibility for the next decade. You can be firm on the numbers and still protect the partnership.`,
                    coach_r3_toosoft:       `<strong>Protecting the relationship that hard is capitulation by another name.</strong> Care for the partnership, yes — but not so much that you soften a −€3.8M finding into something he can live with. The business partner challenges; the pushover agrees.`,
                    coach_r4_corrected:     `<strong>The defensible call.</strong> A corrected, phased €9M Wirtschaftlichkeitsnachweis is exactly what controlling should put its name to — it de-risks the €24M bet, keeps you clear of the covenant cliff, and still lets the COO prove the case if he's right.`,
                    coach_r4_escalate_armed: `<strong>Escalation with airtight evidence holds.</strong> You commissioned the independent estimate and your corrected NPV is negative, so a documented dissent to the board is defensible — you won't be shredded in the room. Pair it with the phased alternative so you're not just saying no.`,
                    coach_r4_escalate_weak: `<strong>You escalated on a guessed ramp and an unverified savings figure.</strong> The COO shreds it in the meeting, you lose the argument AND the relationship, and controlling's credibility drops for a decade. Escalation demands the €40K independent estimate first.`,
                    coach_r4_original:      `<strong>You signed the bias.</strong> The original rests on 6% growth, a vendor's savings deck, and a month-1 ramp. When the line hits 60% of promise and the €24M write-down lands on your covenant test, the board will ask: “controlling signed this — did they not see it?”`,
                    coach_r4_milestones:    `<strong>Milestone gates are the real-options play.</strong> Releasing capital only as savings are proven turns an irreversible bet into a staged commitment — even on a number you don't fully trust, you can say “prove it” instead of betting €24M now.`,
                    coach_r4_allornothing:  `<strong>Why force a binary?</strong> €24M-now-or-kill-it throws away the phased €9M pilot that lets you stage the commitment. All-or-nothing is the riskiest possible structure under this much uncertainty.`,
                    coach_r4_nogov:         `<strong>“A one-off test” is how the rubber stamp returns.</strong> Without a sign-off template that strips sunk costs and forces independent verification, the next champion's optimistic deck sails through. Institutionalise the safeguard.`,

                    sum_default:            `Decision made.`,
                    sum_r1_good:            `Detected before computing; named the case-flipping bias.`,
                    sum_r1_weak:            `Computed first or mis-ranked the biases.`,
                    sum_r2_good:            `Scenario range, sunk cost stripped, independent check.`,
                    sum_r2_weak:            `Left an optimistic or anchored assumption in the model.`,
                    sum_r3_good:            `Conditional yes, led with evidence, rejected the sunk-cost plea.`,
                    sum_r3_capitulate:      `Capitulated to the powerful sponsor.`,
                    sum_r3_weak:            `Held the line only partly under pressure.`,
                    sum_r4_good:            `Corrected, phased sign-off with a governance safeguard.`,
                    sum_r4_original:        `Signed the original €24M proposal as submitted.`,
                    sum_r4_weak:            `Defensible but under-staged or ungoverned.`,

                    insight_assured:        `Rationality assured: you detected the biases before computing, replaced the optimistic anchor with a scenario range, commissioned the independent estimate, stripped the sunk cost, held the line with the sponsor on evidence, and put controlling's name to a corrected, phased sign-off. That is Weber's controlling done right.`,
                    insight_signed:         `You signed the original €24M. The case rested on a vendor's savings deck, 6% growth against a 1.5% order book, and a month-1 ramp — every pathology you were hired to catch. The write-down will land near your covenant test, and the board will ask why controlling didn't see it.`,
                    insight_folded:         `You folded to the sponsor. Endorsing the proposal because the COO is the founder's nephew is precisely the self-interest and power dynamic rationality assurance exists to counteract. The function lost its purpose the moment it caved.`,
                    insight_partial:        `A partial critique. You exposed some of the reasoning failures but left an optimistic or anchored assumption standing, so your corrected case is still contestable. Rank the biases by which line moves the NPV most, and demand the independent check.`,
                    insight_lowrationality: `Your rationality score stayed low — the biases were never fully exposed or tied to the model. Without that, controlling is just a rival forecaster the COO can argue away.`,
                    insight_lowcred:        `Controlling credibility is low. You may be analytically right, but if you pulled rank, deferred, or detonated the relationship, the function can't hold the line on the next decision either.`,
                    insight_detonated:      `You won the argument and lost the sponsor. Being right is not enough — controlling needs a working relationship with the people whose cases it challenges, or it has no influence on the next €24M.`,
                    insight_npvflipped:     `On your corrected assumptions the working NPV turned negative — the case the COO sold as +€5.2M is a loss once the optimism, anchoring, and ramp are fixed. That is the number controlling exists to surface.`,

                    hint_signed:            `You're on track to sign the original — the very proposal built on the vendor's deck and 6% growth. Reconsider: a corrected, phased €9M pilot de-risks the bet without blocking value.`,
                    hint_capitulated:       `You've started to fold to the sponsor. Rationality assurance means challenging the powerful manager's case with evidence, not ratifying it because of who he is.`,
                    hint_detonated:         `You're protecting the relationship too little — a detonated sponsor relationship costs controlling its influence for years. Be firm on the numbers, careful with the person.`,
                    hint_lowrationality:    `Your rationality score is lagging. Name the biases explicitly and tie each to a line in the NPV model before you try to reframe the case.`,
                    hint_strong:            `Strong position — rationality, decision quality and credibility all healthy. Lock it in with a phased sign-off and a governance template so the safeguard outlives this one decision.`,

